cartridge "SACK RACE/JOHN FEDOR/1988"

' SACK RACE
' A ColecoVision game based on "Sack Race" programmed by John Fedor,
' concept by Tom Gauthier and John Fedor, Ahoy! November 1988 (Commodore
' 64, machine code). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by
' Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a byte-for-byte port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/sack-ml.dis), the game is rebuilt for the
' ColecoVision: groceries drawn as tiles that slide a pixel at a time (so
' that seven on one counter never meet the four-sprites-a-line limit), a
' moving belt, a bagger in layered sprites, sound effects.
'
' The game: groceries slide down four checkout counters from the right. You
' are the bagger: walk along the counters and hop from one to another; every
' grocery that reaches your sack is bagged (1 point). One that slides off
' the end of a counter costs a bag (three bags each). Bag 14 x the level's
' number of groceries to finish the level; the groceries come faster every
' second level. Start at any level from 1 to 25 for a bonus of
' 7 x L x (L - 1) points, paid when that first level is finished.
'
' From the original (orig/): four counters; seven grocery places on each;
' five groceries (banana, can, cabbage, ketchup, fish); a try for a new
' grocery every few passes, with a coin toss, on the counters in turn, and
' 3 tries in 8 come to nothing; 14 x level groceries a level; a grocery steps
' a pixel every 25 - min(level, 44) / 2 passes; the bagger walks left and
' right (a pixel every 6 passes) and hops up and down a counter at a time,
' round from the top to the bottom; a grocery is bagged when it is on the
' bagger's counter and left of his sack (behind him too); one past the end
' of a counter loses a bag and ends the turn (the level starts again with
' all its groceries); a finished level goes on with the next one; starting
' level 1-25 for each player and the bonus 7 x L x (L - 1); 1 point a
' grocery; one or two players taking turns after every lost bag; scores of
' seven digits.
'
' Liberties: the screen is redrawn for the 32-column screen (counters from
' column 4, the bagger walks columns 3-20 as on the C64's proportions);
' speeds are in sixtieths of a second (the C64's loop ran about 3.5 passes a
' frame: a grocery moves 806 / (25 - min(L,44) / 2) / 256 pixels a frame,
' the bagger 160 / 256); a new grocery waits until the last one on its
' counter is 24 pixels along (the C64 piled them on top of each other); a
' grocery can always be bagged at the end of the counter (on the C64 the can
' and the bottle fell before the bagger at the far left could reach them,
' a side effect of their sprite margins); a player with no bags left is
' skipped (the C64 gave him more turns); the C64's SHIFT key pause is a
' side button; a computer can play player 2; the lost grocery tumbles off
' the counter and blinks at 2.5 times a second (the C64 flashed it 4 times a
' second); belt, scanner and sounds are new.
'
' Controls: joystick only. Left and right walk, up and down hop to another
' counter, a side button pauses. Player 1 uses controller 1, player 2
' controller 2 (one player: either). On the title, up and down choose,
' left, right or a button change the players, a button starts. On the
' level page up and down choose the starting level, a button is ready.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` after every `clear
' sprites`. Own xorshift random numbers.
'
' Workarounds for Amy bugs sack-bcd-element-carry, sack-div-in-sum, sack-print-bcd-var-index removed 2026-11-01.

